<task id="003" project="messaging-communication-app-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>Phase-0: ACS spike (identity + server-minted chat token + thread + Event Grid round-trip; latency, echo-dedup, publish-size)</title>
    <phase>W0 — Phase 0 Verification + Foundation</phase>
    <status>not-started</status>
    <estimated-hours>8</estimated-hours>
    <dependencies>none</dependencies>
    <blocks>010, 011, 012, 020, 030</blocks>
    <tags>spike, acs</tags>
    <rigor-hint>STANDARD</rigor-hint>
    <rigor-reason>Throwaway exploratory spike proving ACS end-to-end server-side. Output is a findings report + throwaway spike code under `notes/spikes/`, NOT production code — no Step 9.5 gates. But it gates ALL ACS work (010/011/012/020/030), so the findings (latency, echo-dedup, publish-size delta) must be rigorous. STANDARD per §8.</rigor-reason>
    <parallel-group>W0-A</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <fr-refs>—</fr-refs>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <effort>high</effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Phase-0 ACS proving spike (design §12.3, §8). ACS is entirely net-new to Spaarke (design §2 — no `Azure.Communication.*` anywhere). Before committing to the W1/W2 build, PROVE the full server-side round-trip and measure the three numbers the plan says gate the build:

    1. **Create an ACS identity** server-side (BFF trusted-service role) — `createUser` / `createUserAndToken`.
    2. **Mint a `chat`-scoped token server-side** for that identity (uniform server-minting — Entra→ACS exchange is VoIP-only per design §8.2; even internal users get server-minted chat tokens).
    3. **Create a chat thread** with **30-day retention** and **add participants** (`ChatThreadClient.AddParticipants`).
    4. **Send a message** to the thread server-side (`SendMessage`, sender's minted token).
    5. **Capture the message via an Event Grid round-trip** — subscribe to `ChatMessageReceivedInThread`, receive the event (or simulate the documented event schema if a live Event Grid subscription is not provisionable in the spike window), and prove the **subscription-validation handshake** (echo `validationCode`).

    MEASURE and report:
    - **send→persist latency** (send server-side → the Event Grid echo arrives) — the number that tells us the polling cadence (~5s, FR-10/NFR-07) is comfortably above capture latency.
    - **echo-dedup behavior** — our OWN outbound message emits an Event Grid `ChatMessageReceivedInThread`; prove that de-duping on the ACS **message id** makes the echo a no-op (design §4 — the seam that lets outbound stay persist-on-send while inbound stays capture-on-event). Confirm the ACS message id is stable + present on both the send response and the inbound event.
    - **`dotnet publish` size delta** — add `Azure.Communication.Chat` 1.4.0 + `Azure.Communication.Identity` 1.3.1 to a throwaway probe and measure the compressed publish delta vs the ~45.30 MB baseline (design §8.9 claims "negligible, thin over `Azure.Core`" — verify it).

    Use `Azure-Samples/communication-services-authentication-hero-csharp` as the starting point (BFF trusted-service token minting + identity mapping — the blueprint) and `...-dotnet-quickstarts` for thread/participant/send server code. Consult the ACS researcher memory at `.claude/agent-memory/researcher/acs-chat-integration-2026-07-16.md` (if absent, run the `researcher` subagent to (re)establish ACS findings and note the gap). This is THROWAWAY spike code under `notes/spikes/` — NOT production `Services/Acs/`. Output findings to `notes/spikes/003-acs-spike.md`.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Spaarke BFF integration engineer proving a net-new Azure transport. Fluent in the ACS trusted-service model (BFF mints identities + tokens; clients hold none), `Azure.Communication.Identity` / `Azure.Communication.Chat`, Event Grid subscription-validation + at-least-once delivery, and the Spaarke publish-size discipline (root §10 / NFR-01). Spike posture — prove and measure, throwaway code.
  </role>

  <goal>
    A findings report at `notes/spikes/003-acs-spike.md` proving the server-side ACS round-trip (identity → chat token → thread w/ 30-day retention → send → Event Grid capture + validation handshake) with three measured numbers: send→persist latency, echo-dedup confirmation (stable ACS message id dedupes the own-echo), and the `dotnet publish` size delta for the two ACS SDKs. W1/W2 (010/011/012/020/030) can be built against proven behavior and confirmed footprint rather than assumptions.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      Design §13 R-1 names ACS being entirely net-new as the top risk; §12.3 mandates this spike first. The three measured numbers each de-risk a specific downstream decision: latency validates the ~5s poll (§6.2/NFR-07); echo-dedup validates the persist-on-send + capture-on-event coexistence (§4 — the whole outbound/inbound model rests on the ACS message id being a stable idempotency key); publish-size validates the §8.9/NFR-01 claim before the SDKs are committed to the production project (root §10 ceiling ≤60 MB, ≥+5 MB single-task delta requires justification).

      §11 Component Justification: no production component is introduced here — this is throwaway spike code. The production ACS components it de-risks (identity/token minting in 010, thread ops in 011, provisioning in 012) carry their own justification. Cost-of-doing-nothing for THIS task: building 010/011/012/020/030 on unverified ACS behavior — e.g., discovering mid-W3 that the echo is not dedupable (breaking §4) or that the SDK footprint blows the size ceiling.
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file>projects/messaging-communication-app-r1/design.md (§4 flows/echo-dedup, §8 ACS integration, §8.9 footprint, §12.3 spike mandate)</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Sprk.Bff.Api.csproj (baseline package set — for the throwaway publish-size probe)</file>
      <file>src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Communication/ (existing channel + job patterns the production build will reuse — reference only, do not modify in the spike)</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">THROWAWAY spike — spike code lives under `notes/spikes/` (per template §"What goes in notes"). MUST NOT add production code to `Services/Acs/` or `Services/Communication/`; MUST NOT commit the ACS SDK refs into `Sprk.Bff.Api.csproj` (measure in a throwaway probe project/branch and revert).</constraint>
    <constraint source="design §8.2">Token minting is SERVER-SIDE only; the spike proves uniform server-minting (`chat` scope). No client-side ACS SDK, no token to a browser (NFR-04).</constraint>
    <constraint source="design §8.3">Event Grid is at-least-once + unordered + may duplicate → prove idempotent dedupe on the ACS message id (the echo-dedup measurement). Prove the subscription-validation handshake (echo `validationCode`).</constraint>
    <constraint source="design §8.7">Thread created with 30-day retention (or explicit delete-post-persist) — verify the retention setting is honoured at create.</constraint>
    <constraint source="root §10 / NFR-01">Report the `dotnet publish -c Release` compressed size delta for `Azure.Communication.Chat` 1.4.0 + `Azure.Communication.Identity` 1.3.1 vs the ~45.30 MB baseline; flag if ≥+5 MB (design §8.9 predicts negligible).</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-028">Any credential in the spike uses central `TokenCredential` patterns; MUST NOT `new` a `ConfidentialClientApplication` (even in throwaway code, model the production auth path).</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">If a live ACS resource / Event Grid subscription cannot be provisioned in the spike window, simulate the documented event schema + validation handshake and clearly label which numbers are MEASURED vs ESTIMATED-from-docs; do not block the project — deliver the report with the gap noted.</constraint>
  </constraints>

