    <patterns>
      <pattern name="ACS trusted-service token minting" location="Azure-Samples/communication-services-authentication-hero-csharp">
        BFF creates ACS identities + mints scoped tokens; identity mapped to the app's user store. The blueprint for task 010.
      </pattern>
      <pattern name="ACS thread/participant/send (server)" location="Azure-Samples/communication-services-dotnet-quickstarts">
        `ChatThreadClient` create-thread / AddParticipants / SendMessage server code. The blueprint for task 011/020.
      </pattern>
      <pattern name="Event Grid subscription-validation handshake" location="design.md §8.3">
        Echo the `validationCode` on the SubscriptionValidationEvent; then idempotent capture on ACS message id. The blueprint for task 030/031.
      </pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps>
    <step order="1">Read `design.md` §4/§8/§8.9/§12.3 + the ACS researcher memory (if present; else run `researcher` to (re)establish findings and note the gap). Read `.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md` for the publish-size procedure.</step>
    <step order="2">Stand up a throwaway spike (console app or a scratch branch/probe under `notes/spikes/`) referencing `Azure.Communication.Identity` 1.3.1 + `Azure.Communication.Chat` 1.4.0. Use the auth-hero-csharp sample as the identity/token skeleton.</step>
    <step order="3">Prove identity + token: `createUserAndToken(["chat"])` server-side; capture the `communicationUserId` and the token scope/lifetime (1–24h). Confirm no token would need to reach a client.</step>
    <step order="4">Prove thread: create a chat thread with 30-day retention; `AddParticipants`; `SendMessage`. Capture the ACS message id from the send response.</step>
    <step order="5">Prove capture: subscribe an Event Grid handler (or simulate the documented `ChatMessageReceivedInThread` schema); prove the subscription-validation handshake (echo `validationCode`); receive the event for the sent message; confirm the ACS message id on the event matches the send response (echo-dedup key).</step>
    <step order="6">MEASURE send→persist latency (send → event arrival). MEASURE echo-dedup: assert de-duping on the ACS message id makes the own-echo a no-op. Label MEASURED vs ESTIMATED where a live subscription was unavailable.</step>
    <step order="7">MEASURE publish-size: `dotnet publish -c Release` a probe with vs without the two ACS SDKs; report the compressed delta vs ~45.30 MB. Revert any probe changes to `Sprk.Bff.Api.csproj`.</step>
    <step order="8">Write `notes/spikes/003-acs-spike.md`: the proven round-trip, the three numbers, the recommended poll cadence given the latency, the echo-dedup key confirmation, the size delta + go/no-go vs the ceiling, and a "consumed by 010/011/012/030/031" section mapping each finding to the production task it de-risks.</step>
    <step order="9">Delete/quarantine the throwaway spike code under `notes/spikes/`; confirm no ACS refs leaked into `Sprk.Bff.Api.csproj`. Update `tasks/TASK-INDEX.md` (003 ✅) + `current-task.md`.</step>
  </steps>

